            <ArticleTitle>Laughing headache with giant pacchionian granulations.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>681-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/head.12056</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Laughing is recognized as a provoking factor for headache, certainly underestimated among the general population and few cases have been published to date. We report a single case of severe headache, provoked almost exclusively by outbursts of laughing, where venous magnetic resonance imaging revealed the presence of giant Pacchioni granulations in both right and transverse sinuses. Reviewing published cases of laughing headache, we discuss possible mechanisms of pain and the role of giant Pacchionian granulations.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2013 American Headache Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
